        "text": "A scalper offers this liquidity to the market and makes money off the spread. For example, Bob wants to sell cryptocurrency A for $10, but scalpers and other buyers want it for the lower price of $9.95. Bob offers the crypto to the market at $10, but he doesn't get any takers. Bob's in a rush to sell, so he chooses to sell it for $9.95 to a scalper. Another investor, Alice, wants to purchase cryptocurrency A for $9.95, but sellers and scalpers only offer to sell it at $10. Alice is also in a r that she will meet the scalper's offers and purchase cryptocurrency A for $10. The scalper uses the crypto he bought from Bob for $9.95 and sells it to Alice for $10. The scalper makes a profit of 5 cents on each unit of Cryptocurrency A that he sells. This is a small amount, but they can make larger profits if they do this transaction over hundreds of thousands of units.",
